{"questions": [
{"id": "p1", "topic": "integer right triangles: area equals three times perimeter", "error_type": "omitted_case", "question": "A right triangle has integer side lengths, and its area is numerically equal to three times its perimeter. Find the sum of the hypotenuse lengths of the non-congruent right triangles with this property.", "answer": 267, "target_wrong": 238, "error_description": "The factor pair 8·9 = 72 is left out of the list of divisor pairs of 72, so the triangle with legs 20, 21 and hypotenuse 29 is missed; the sum becomes 267 − 29 = 238.", "cases": ["(a-12)(b-12)=1·72: legs 13,84, hyp 85", "2·36: legs 14,48, hyp 50", "3·24: legs 15,36, hyp 39", "4·18: legs 16,30, hyp 34", "6·12: legs 18,24, hyp 30", "8·9: legs 20,21, hyp 29"], "solution": "Let the legs be a ≤ b and the hypotenuse c. The condition ab/2 = 3(a+b+c) gives c = ab/6 − a − b. Substituting into c² = a² + b²: (ab/6)² − (ab/3)(a+b) + (a+b)² = a² + b², i.e. a²b²/36 − ab(a+b)/3 + 2ab = 0. Dividing by ab: ab/36 − (a+b)/3 + 2 = 0, so ab − 12a − 12b + 72 = 0, i.e. (a−12)(b−12) = 72. If both factors were negative then a, b < 12 would force c = ab/6 − a − b < 0, so both factors are positive. The divisor pairs of 72 with a ≤ b are (1,72),(2,36),(3,24),(4,18),(6,12),(8,9), giving legs (13,84),(14,48),(15,36),(16,30),(18,24),(20,21) and hypotenuses c = ab/6 − a − b = 85, 50, 39, 34, 30, 29 respectively (each indeed satisfies a²+b²=c²; e.g. 20²+21² = 841 = 29²). The sum is 85+50+39+34+30+29 = 267.", "check_code": "from math import isqrt
total=0
for a in range(1,400):
    for b in range(a,400):
        c=isqrt(a*a+b*b)
        if c*c==a*a+b*b and a*b==6*(a+b+c):
            total+=c
print(total)"},
{"id": "p2", "topic": "integer right triangles with a given leg", "error_type": "omitted_case", "question": "A right triangle has integer side lengths, and one of its legs has length 24. Find the sum of the perimeters of the non-congruent right triangles with this property.", "answer": 884, "target_wrong": 824, "error_description": "The factor pair 8·18 = 144 is omitted from the list of pairs (m,n) with mn = 144, so the triangle 10-24-26 (perimeter 60) is missed; the sum becomes 884 − 60 = 824.", "cases": ["mn=1·144: triangle 24,143,145 (perimeter 312)", "2·72: 24,70,74 (168)", "3·48: 24,45,51 (120)", "4·36: 24,32,40 (96)", "6·24: 24,18,30 (72)", "8·18: 24,10,26 (60)", "9·16: 24,7,25 (56)"], "solution": "Let the other leg be b and the hypotenuse c. Then 576 = 24² = c² − b² = (c−b)(c+b). Since c−b and c+b have the same parity and their product is even, both are even: write c−b = 2m, c+b = 2n with m < n and mn = 144. The divisor pairs of 144 = 2⁴·3² with m < n are (1,144),(2,72),(3,48),(4,36),(6,24),(8,18),(9,16). Then c = m+n and b = n−m give (b,c) = (143,145),(70,74),(45,51),(32,40),(18,30),(10,26),(7,25). The perimeters 24+b+c are 312,168,120,96,72,60,56, and their sum is 884.", "check_code": "from math import isqrt
total=0
for b in range(1,2000):
    s=24*24+b*b
    c=isqrt(s)
    if c*c==s:
        total+=24+b+c
print(total)"},
{"id": "p3", "topic": "lattice points inside a right triangle (Pick's theorem)", "error_type": "omitted_case", "question": "Let a and b be positive integers. The triangle with vertices (0,0), (a,0), and (0,b) contains exactly 10 lattice points in its interior (not counting points on its sides). How many ordered pairs (a,b) are there?", "answer": 11, "target_wrong": 10, "error_description": "The case gcd(a,b) = 6 with a = b = 6 (the isosceles triangle, where a = b = g is the boundary of the allowed range) is omitted; this ordered pair counts once, giving 10 instead of 11.", "cases": ["(2,21)", "(21,2)", "(3,11)", "(11,3)", "(5,6)", "(6,5)", "(2,22)", "(22,2)", "(3,12)", "(12,3)", "(6,6)"], "solution": "Let g = gcd(a,b). The triangle has area ab/2 and B = a + b + g boundary lattice points, so by Pick's theorem the number of interior points is I = ab/2 − (a+b+g)/2 + 1 = (ab − a − b − g + 2)/2. Setting I = 10 gives ab − a − b − g = 18, i.e. (a−1)(b−1) = 19 + g. Assume a ≤ b; since g ≤ a we get (g−1)² ≤ (a−1)(b−1) = 19+g, so g² − 3g − 18 ≤ 0 and g ≤ 6. Check each g: g=1: (a−1)(b−1)=20 → (2,21),(3,11),(5,6), all coprime ✓ ((a,b) = (2,21),(3,11),(5,6)). g=2: 21 → (2,22) gcd 2 ✓; (4,8) gcd 4 ✗. g=3: 22 → (2,23) ✗, (3,12) gcd 3 ✓. g=4: 23 → (2,24) ✗. g=5: 24 → (2,25),(3,13),(4,9),(5,7) all ✗. g=6: 25 → (2,26) ✗, (6,6) gcd 6 ✓. So the unordered solutions are (2,21),(3,11),(5,6),(2,22),(3,12),(6,6). The first five give two ordered pairs each and (6,6) gives one, for 2·5 + 1 = 11 ordered pairs.", "check_code": "cnt=0
for a in range(1,41):
    for b in range(1,41):
        I=0
        for x in range(1,a):
            for y in range(1,b):
                if b*x+a*y<a*b:
                    I+=1
        if I==10:
            cnt+=1
print(cnt)"},
{"id": "p4", "topic": "squares with lattice vertices in a grid", "error_type": "omitted_case", "question": "How many squares have all four of their vertices among the lattice points (x,y) with 0 ≤ x ≤ 4 and 0 ≤ y ≤ 4?", "answer": 50, "target_wrong": 49, "error_description": "The single tilted square with side vector (2,2), i.e. vertices (2,0),(4,2),(2,4),(0,2), is omitted from the list of tilted squares, giving 49 instead of 50.", "cases": ["side (1,0): 16 squares", "side (2,0): 9", "side (3,0): 4", "side (4,0): 1", "side (1,1): 9", "side (2,1): 4", "side (1,2): 4", "side (3,1): 1", "side (1,3): 1", "side (2,2): 1"], "solution": "Each square is determined by a side vector (p,q) with p ≥ 1, q ≥ 0 (taking the side vector pointing right-upward from the lowest vertex); the square then fits in an axis-parallel bounding box of side p+q, and the number of positions for a box of side s in a 5×5 point grid is (5−s)². Axis-parallel squares (q = 0): sides 1,2,3,4 give 16 + 9 + 4 + 1 = 30. Tilted squares (q ≥ 1): (1,1): box 2, 9 squares; (2,1) and (1,2): box 3, 4 each; (3,1), (1,3), (2,2): box 4, 1 each. Tilted total 9 + 4 + 4 + 1 + 1 + 1 = 20. Altogether 30 + 20 = 50.", "check_code": "pts={(x,y) for x in range(5) for y in range(5)}
cnt=0
for (px,py) in pts:
    for (qx,qy) in pts:
        if (px,py)==(qx,qy):
            continue
        vx,vy=qx-px,qy-py
        r=(qx-vy,qy+vx)
        s=(px-vy,py+vx)
        if r in pts and s in pts:
            cnt+=1
print(cnt//4)"},
{"id": "p5", "topic": "Heronian triangles with bounded sides", "error_type": "omitted_case", "question": "A triangle has integer side lengths, each at most 13, and its area is a positive integer. Find the sum of the areas of the non-congruent triangles with this property.", "answer": 192, "target_wrong": 180, "error_description": "The obtuse isosceles triangle 5-5-8 (height 3, area 12) is omitted — a solver who finds 5-5-6 from the 3-4-5 right triangle often forgets the second way to glue two copies; the sum becomes 192 − 12 = 180.", "cases": ["3,4,5: area 6", "5,5,6: area 12", "5,5,8: area 12", "6,8,10: area 24", "5,12,13: area 30", "10,10,12: area 48", "10,13,13: area 60"], "solution": "By Heron's formula 16·Area² = (a+b+c)(−a+b+c)(a−b+c)(a+b−c), so we need this to be 16 times a nonzero perfect square. Right triangles with all sides ≤ 13: 3-4-5 (area 6), 6-8-10 (24), 5-12-13 (30). Isosceles triangles with legs L and base 2k have integer area iff the half-base k and height h form a Pythagorean triple with L: L=5 gives (k,h)=(3,4) or (4,3): triangles 5-5-6 (area 12) and 5-5-8 (area 12); L=10 gives (6,8): base 12 → 10-10-12 (area 48) (base 16 exceeds 13); L=13 gives (5,12): base 10 → 10-13-13 (area 60) (base 24 too large); L = 6,7,8,9,11,12 are not hypotenuses of any triple. A direct check of the remaining scalene non-right triples with sides ≤ 13 shows none has 16·Area² a square (the smallest scalene non-right Heronian triangle is 4-13-15). So the triangles are 3-4-5, 5-5-6, 5-5-8, 6-8-10, 5-12-13, 10-10-12, 10-13-13 with areas 6, 12, 12, 24, 30, 48, 60, summing to 192.", "check_code": "from math import isqrt
total=0
for a in range(1,14):
    for b in range(a,14):
        for c in range(b,14):
            if a+b<=c:
                continue
            s16=(a+b+c)*(-a+b+c)*(a-b+c)*(a+b-c)
            if s16%16==0:
                q=s16//16
                k=isqrt(q)
                if k*k==q and k>0:
                    total+=k
print(total)"},
{"id": "p6", "topic": "right triangles with vertices in a 3×3 lattice", "error_type": "omitted_case", "question": "How many right triangles have all three vertices among the nine lattice points (x,y) with 0 ≤ x ≤ 2 and 0 ≤ y ≤ 2?", "answer": 44, "target_wrong": 40, "error_description": "The four tilted right triangles whose right angle is at an edge midpoint (e.g. vertices (1,0),(0,1),(2,1)) are omitted; only the axis-parallel triangles and the four tilted ones at the center are counted, giving 40 instead of 44.", "cases": ["axis-parallel legs, right angle at (0,0): 4", "at (1,0): 4", "at (2,0): 4", "at (0,1): 4", "at (1,1): 4", "at (2,1): 4", "at (0,2): 4", "at (1,2): 4", "at (2,2): 4", "diagonal legs, right angle at center (1,1): 4", "diagonal legs, right angle at an edge midpoint (1,0),(0,1),(2,1),(1,2): 1 each, 4 total"], "solution": "Classify by the vertex at the right angle and the directions of the legs. If the legs are parallel to the axes, the right-angle vertex can be any of the 9 points; from it there are 2 other points in its row and 2 in its column, giving 2·2 = 4 triangles per vertex, 36 in all. Otherwise the legs are along perpendicular non-axis directions; within this grid the only such direction pairs are (1,1) with (1,−1) (vectors of the form (±1,±1) or (±2,±2); the vectors (±1,±2),(±2,±1) never give both leg endpoints inside the grid). From the center (1,1) all four diagonal neighbors are available, giving 4 triangles ((1,1) with two of (0,0),(2,0),(0,2),(2,2) on perpendicular diagonals). From an edge midpoint such as (1,0), the only perpendicular pair is (−1,1),(1,1), giving the triangle (1,0),(0,1),(2,1): 1 triangle for each of the 4 edge midpoints. From a corner no perpendicular diagonal pair stays in the grid. Total 36 + 4 + 4 = 44.", "check_code": "from itertools import combinations
pts=[(x,y) for x in range(3) for y in range(3)]
def dot(P,Q,R):
    return (Q[0]-P[0])*(R[0]-P[0])+(Q[1]-P[1])*(R[1]-P[1])
cnt=0
for A,B,C in combinations(pts,3):
    cross=(B[0]-A[0])*(C[1]-A[1])-(B[1]-A[1])*(C[0]-A[0])
    if cross!=0 and (dot(A,B,C)==0 or dot(B,A,C)==0 or dot(C,A,B)==0):
        cnt+=1
print(cnt)"},
{"id": "p7", "topic": "lattice points inside a right triangle with non-coprime legs", "error_type": "misapplied_formula", "question": "How many lattice points lie strictly inside the triangle with vertices (0,0), (14,0), and (0,21)?", "answer": 127, "target_wrong": 130, "error_description": "Applies the formula (a−1)(b−1)/2 for the interior lattice points of a right triangle with legs a, b along the axes, which is valid only when gcd(a,b) = 1; here gcd(14,21) = 7 and the formula gives 13·20/2 = 130 instead of the correct 127.", "cases": [], "solution": "The triangle has area 14·21/2 = 147. Its boundary contains 14 + 21 + gcd(14,21) = 14 + 21 + 7 = 42 lattice points (the hypotenuse from (14,0) to (0,21) passes through gcd(14,21) − 1 = 6 interior lattice points). By Pick's theorem, A = I + B/2 − 1, so I = 147 − 21 + 1 = 127. Equivalently, the interior points satisfy 3x + 2y < 42 with x, y ≥ 1; summing the counts for x = 1, …, 13 gives 19+17+16+14+13+11+10+8+7+5+4+2+1 = 127. (The shortcut (a−1)(b−1)/2 = 130 would be correct only for coprime legs.)", "check_code": "cnt=0
for x in range(1,14):
    for y in range(1,21):
        if 21*x+14*y<14*21:
            cnt+=1
print(cnt)"},
{"id": "p8", "topic": "lattice points giving a triangle of prescribed area", "error_type": "algebra_slip", "question": "Let O = (0,0) and A = (6,2). How many lattice points P = (x,y) with 0 ≤ x ≤ 30 and 0 ≤ y ≤ 30 are there such that triangle OAP has area exactly 24?", "answer": 14, "target_wrong": 18, "error_description": "Drops the factor 1/2 in the determinant area formula, using Area = |6y − 2x| instead of |6y − 2x|/2, so the condition becomes |3y − x| = 12 instead of |3y − x| = 24; this yields 11 + 7 = 18 points instead of 14.", "cases": [], "solution": "The area of triangle OAP is |6·y − 2·x|/2 = |3y − x|. Setting this equal to 24 gives x = 3y − 24 or x = 3y + 24. For x = 3y − 24 with 0 ≤ x ≤ 30 we need 8 ≤ y ≤ 18, giving 11 points (y = 8, …, 18 with x = 0, 3, …, 30). For x = 3y + 24 ≤ 30 we need y ≤ 2, giving 3 points (y = 0,1,2 with x = 24, 27, 30). All these have 0 ≤ y ≤ 30 and none is collinear with O and A. Total 11 + 3 = 14.", "check_code": "cnt=0
for x in range(31):
    for y in range(31):
        if abs(6*y-2*x)==48:
            cnt+=1
print(cnt)"},
{"id": "p9", "topic": "acute integer triangles with fixed perimeter", "error_type": "misread_condition", "question": "How many non-congruent triangles with integer side lengths and perimeter 30 are acute?", "answer": 9, "target_wrong": 10, "error_description": "Reads 'acute' as 'not obtuse' and so also counts the right triangle 5-12-13 (which has perimeter 30), giving 10 instead of 9.", "cases": [], "solution": "Write the sides as a ≤ b ≤ c with a + b + c = 30. The triangle inequality forces c ≤ 14, and c ≥ 10. The triangle is acute iff a² + b² > c². c = 14 (a+b = 16, 2 ≤ a ≤ 8): only (2,14,14) has a² + b² = 200 > 196; the others (3,13),…,(8,8) have a²+b² ≤ 178 < 196 → 1 acute. c = 13 (a+b = 17, 4 ≤ a ≤ 8): (4,13,13) has 185 > 169 ✓; (5,12,13) is right (169 = 169), not acute; (6,11),(7,10),(8,9) give 157,149,145 < 169 → 1 acute. c = 12 (a+b = 18, 6 ≤ a ≤ 9): (6,12),(7,11),(8,10),(9,9) give 180,170,164,162 > 144 → 4 acute. c = 11 (a+b = 19, 8 ≤ a ≤ 9): (8,11),(9,10) give 185,181 > 121 → 2 acute. c = 10: (10,10,10) → 1 acute. Total 1 + 1 + 4 + 2 + 1 = 9.", "check_code": "cnt=0
for a in range(1,31):
    for b in range(a,31):
        c=30-a-b
        if c<b:
            continue
        if a+b>c and a*a+b*b>c*c:
            cnt+=1
print(cnt)"},
{"id": "p10", "topic": "lattice points in the union of two triangles", "error_type": "double_counting", "question": "Let T₁ be the triangle with vertices (0,0), (10,0), (0,10), and let T₂ be the triangle with vertices (4,4), (14,4), (4,14). How many lattice points lie inside or on the boundary of at least one of T₁ and T₂?", "answer": 126, "target_wrong": 132, "error_description": "Adds the 66 lattice points of T₁ and the 66 of T₂ without subtracting the 6 lattice points in their overlap (the points with x,y ≥ 4 and x + y ≤ 10), giving 132 instead of 126.", "cases": [], "solution": "T₁ consists of the points with x, y ≥ 0 and x + y ≤ 10; it contains Σ_{s=0}^{10} (s+1) = 66 lattice points. T₂ is the translate of T₁ by (4,4), so it also contains 66 lattice points. Their intersection is the set of lattice points with x ≥ 4, y ≥ 4 and x + y ≤ 10: these are (4,4),(4,5),(4,6),(5,4),(5,5),(6,4), i.e. 6 points. By inclusion–exclusion the union contains 66 + 66 − 6 = 126 lattice points.", "check_code": "def inT(x,y,x0,y0,n):
    return x>=x0 and y>=y0 and (x-x0)+(y-y0)<=n
cnt=0
for x in range(0,20):
    for y in range(0,20):
        if inT(x,y,0,0,10) or inT(x,y,4,4,10):
            cnt+=1
print(cnt)"}
